{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/847509-looking-to-get-back-into-the-game","id":847509,"title":"Looking to get back into the game","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-03-14T20:39:06-07:00","id":847509,"title":"Looking to get back into the game","content":"Hello to all.<br /> <br /> I always liked werewolf Forsaken, 8 years ago it was my first RPG that i ever DM. However back them i DM a very .....particular version of the game. Barely any spirits, killing Pure left and right. Yeaaaah it was our version of trenchcoat and katanas.<br /> <br /> Fast forwards to today, i just finished an fairly awesome game of Werewolf The apocalypse, i had very bad misconceptions about the game and the DM of that just shattered all of them and make the game and the setting great. From a 90s metal album muderhobbo idea of the game he made werewolf into a very political heavy game full of humanity.<br /> <br /> So i kinda think i owe another shot at the game. And while i like the game and the setting and own some books (Core, blood of the wolf and tribes of the moon) there are elements that i find.....obtuse or problematic to run a game with.<br /> <br /> Things i like: The rules, variety of enemies, the urban shaman feel to the game and variety of chargen (partial changes, customizable wolf form, the purpose of forms themselves). The fact that fetishes arent rare and can be made easily....sorta.<br /> <br /> What i struggle with:<br /> <br /> 1) How uratha society works? For what i understand the game sells me the idea that pack are insolated on their territory and they dont cooperate with other packs because thats a signs of weakness and could lead to loosing the territory. That ranks doesnt have any pull because each pack handles their territory. This makes making NPCs difficult because i feel every other pack is by default antagonistic in a binary way. &quot;Are they gonna try to steal our turf? No, then when do they leave? Yes, then can we beat them? Yes, then we beat them. No, then we loose the turf&quot;.<br /> <br /> 2) Spirit courts. What do they do? I get the ranks and pecking but not what it means for a spririt to be under another court wise. How they function and why spirits join one.<br /> <br /> 3) This is related to 1) but how do werewolf learn the ropes? Why would a more experience werewolf teach new ones the ropes? Isnt that raising the competition?<br /> <br /> 4) Is the rookies &quot;city book&quot; any good for showing how to set up a city? I ask because Miami for changeling and Boston for mage are the most &quot;outside the box&quot; cities and not really examples of standard &quot;splat city&quot;.<br /> <br /> 5) Tribes are meaningless socially wise? Or at least thats the vibe i got from the game. When pack comes before anything else by defaults then what rank and tribe factor in socially wise?<br /> <br /> What i dont like.<br /> <br /> The human identity. I dont hate it per say and im certantly not oppose to a player wanting to explore that but i find the whole &quot;Peter Parker secret identity&quot; incredible dull. I think the human characters are more interesting in their reactions to the supernatural that pulling the &quot;we are forever apart from humanity&quot; done to the dead. Something i did like about WtA was the garou nation which made it easier to ignore the &quot;secret ID&quot; thing. Is there something similar in WtF 2nd?<br /> <br /> My idea is running a game from the first change since all the players dont know anything about the setting.<br /> <br /> Thanks for any answer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","user":"LokiRavenSpeak","date":". 03-14-2016, 11:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-14T21:52:20-07:00","id":847538,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/847509-looking-to-get-back-into-the-game#post847509\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1) How uratha society works? For what i understand the game sells me the idea that pack are insolated on their territory and they dont cooperate with other packs because thats a signs of weakness and could lead to loosing the territory. That ranks doesnt have any pull because each pack handles their territory. This makes making NPCs difficult because i feel every other pack is by default antagonistic in a binary way. &quot;Are they gonna try to steal our turf? No, then when do they leave? Yes, then can we beat them? Yes, then we beat them. No, then we loose the turf&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know where you got that idea. Packs often join together with other packs and create protectorates, essentially small 'nations' of packs. Werewolves aren't all concerned about weakness. In addition, packs will usually have to deal with other packs because their prey isn't going to care as much about territorial boundaries, so you're going to find yourself trespassing sooner or later. And of course there are occasionally the elements that require multiple packs to deal with. These encounters aren't really antagonistic, though, they're opportunities.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2) Spirit courts. What do they do? I get the ranks and pecking but not what it means for a spririt to be under another court wise. How they function and why spirits join one. </div> </div> </div>Spirits join them out of necessity. The hisil is predatory, if you aren't going to serve a court and offer up Essence tithes to the higher-ups, you're going to be fodder. They tend to focus on their own 'business,' usually related to their Resonance and increasing it throughout the area, often trying to get werewolves or humans to assist. A powerful city spirit will have a court of lesser, related spirits, like streets and buildings and businesses that are probably powerful in their own right but only as a facet of the city spirit.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3) This is related to 1) but how do werewolf learn the ropes? Why would a more experience werewolf teach new ones the ropes? Isnt that raising the competition? </div> </div> </div>The best and most common way is for other werewolves to teach them. Usually the werewolf that first finds you after a First Change will, at least, direct you to someone who can show you the ropes. And at some point you'll be dealing with someone from a tribe who will probably run you through an initiation and teach you a lot more. Some werewolves are from long lineages, and have elders who look out for newly changed in their families. But any experienced werewolf who stumbles upon a newly changed werewolf has reason to teach them something. If they don't, the new werewolf is likely to become a pawn to manipulative spirits or some enemy faction, not to mention the death rages they might get into, which would in turn draw the attention of law enforcement and werewolf hunters and werewolves who view you as irresponsible for not telling a new werewolf about the Oath of the Moon and that, 'The Herd must not know.' Competition isn't really a thing the way you make it out to be.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 4) Is the rookies &quot;city book&quot; any good for showing how to set up a city? I ask because Miami for changeling and Boston for mage are the most &quot;outside the box&quot; cities and not really examples of standard &quot;splat city&quot;. </div> </div> </div>It's better for a wide area than a city. The werewolf population is still low after the idigam attack, and the packs that do exist are pretty spread out. It's the Rockies, after all, not just Denver. But beyond that, yeah, it's pretty good. So is the Santa Fe one, in the back of the Pure book, though that focuses mostly on the Pure. It's worth keeping in mind when looking through either that werewolves have changed a lot across editions.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 5) Tribes are meaningless socially wise? Or at least thats the vibe i got from the game. When pack comes before anything else by defaults then what rank and tribe factor in socially wise? </div> </div> </div>Pack doesn't always come before everything else. That would be nice but it's not a guarantee. Tribes are meaningless in the same way your high school, college and workplace are. There where you made connections and have things in common with people, whether you just happened to go to the same place or had the same mentor as someone else, or you do the same thing 'for a living.' Just by meeting another member of the same tribe doesn't mean you'll be great friends but it likely means you'll share some common interests and connections.<br /> <br /> You said 'rank' a few times, I don't know what you mean by that. I'm guessing you don't mean honorary spirit rank. Are you talking about alpha, beta, omega stuff?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What i dont like.<br /> <br /> The human identity. I dont hate it per say and im certantly not oppose to a player wanting to explore that but i find the whole &quot;Peter Parker secret identity&quot; incredible dull. I think the human characters are more interesting in their reactions to the supernatural that pulling the &quot;we are forever apart from humanity&quot; done to the dead. Something i did like about WtA was the garou nation which made it easier to ignore the &quot;secret ID&quot; thing. Is there something similar in WtF 2nd? </div> </div> </div>You don't have to have much of a human life, but it's the kind of thing that can keep you in touch with your territory. And since many pack members are going to be wolf-blooded and human, having a human identity can give you reason to keep in touch with them. Packs can be businesses, with a few werewolves in charge and a couple wolf-blooded in the know and many human employees with no idea there's a supernatural aspect to their workplace. They can be criminal organizations where only the 'made men,' mostly wolf-blooded, know the truth about the bosses. Taking the human side of that out doesn't give you much of an anchor in the fleshly side of the game. It's still something you can do, though, you'll just likely hover around lower Harmony.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My idea is running a game from the first change since all the players dont know anything about the setting. </div> </div> </div>I'd really suggest against starting a game before a first change if you want to toss out the identity of the player character, but that's up to you. The fiction in the second edition book basically talks about a pack of werewolves who have to give up their identities for the hunt, and it's not an easy thing to do.<br /> <br /> Hope we can help with your game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-15T00:42:06-07:00","id":847570,"title":"","content":"It sounds like most (all) of your perceptions are from 1st edition. Have a read through 2nd edition, it fixes the problems people had with aimlessness and 'what do werewolves do'. It should explain most of what you're asking here.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Writer. Developer.</i> <b>World of Darkness</b> | <b>Chronicles of Darkness</b> | <b>The Trinity Continuum</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/156-bunyip","name":"Bunyip","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1880"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-15T13:20:41-07:00","id":847822,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/847509-looking-to-get-back-into-the-game?p=847538#post847538\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't know where you got that idea. Packs often join together with other packs and create protectorates, essentially small 'nations' of packs. Werewolves aren't all concerned about weakness. In addition, packs will usually have to deal with other packs because their prey isn't going to care as much about territorial boundaries, so you're going to find yourself trespassing sooner or later. And of course there are occasionally the elements that require multiple packs to deal with. These encounters aren't really antagonistic, though, they're opportunities.</div> </div> </div> </div>I dont remember exactly when but it was the vibe i got from when i read the core from 1st edition. I remember reading stuff like packs not venturing the territory of other werewolves without permision because it was considered a challenge.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/847509-looking-to-get-back-into-the-game?p=847538#post847538\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Spirits join them out of necessity. The hisil is predatory, if you aren't going to serve a court and offer up Essence tithes to the higher-ups, you're going to be fodder. They tend to focus on their own 'business,' usually related to their Resonance and increasing it throughout the area, often trying to get werewolves or humans to assist. A powerful city spirit will have a court of lesser, related spirits, like streets and buildings and businesses that are probably powerful in their own right but only as a facet of the city spirit.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ahh so its a tribute based thing. When Spirits give essense to their superiors for protection? What about hybrid spirits, i remember reading they were abomination in 1st edition but it seems that now they are pretty common in 2nd?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/847509-looking-to-get-back-into-the-game?p=847538#post847538\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The best and most common way is for other werewolves to teach them. Usually the werewolf that first finds you after a First Change will, at least, direct you to someone who can show you the ropes. And at some point you'll be dealing with someone from a tribe who will probably run you through an initiation and teach you a lot more. Some werewolves are from long lineages, and have elders who look out for newly changed in their families. But any experienced werewolf who stumbles upon a newly changed werewolf has reason to teach them something. If they don't, the new werewolf is likely to become a pawn to manipulative spirits or some enemy faction, not to mention the death rages they might get into, which would in turn draw the attention of law enforcement and werewolf hunters and werewolves who view you as irresponsible for not telling a new werewolf about the Oath of the Moon and that, 'The Herd must not know.' Competition isn't really a thing the way you make it out to be.</div> </div> </div> </div>So how does a new pack gets a territory? I mean, it is in setting for a more experience pack to say &quot;yeah....take that part of our territory, we are overworked as it is&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/847509-looking-to-get-back-into-the-game?p=847538#post847538\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You said 'rank' a few times, I don't know what you mean by that. I'm guessing you don't mean honorary spirit rank. Are you talking about alpha, beta, omega stuff?</div> </div> </div> </div>I meant rank as in renown. For example i remember reading a tale in Signs of the moon, the elodoth chapter, in which 2 werewolves went to a higher ranked Elodoth for a dispute between a wolf blooded. Which always seem contrived to me, wouldnt challenge be more straight forward?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/847509-looking-to-get-back-into-the-game?p=847538#post847538\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You don't have to have much of a human life, but it's the kind of thing that can keep you in touch with your territory. And since many pack members are going to be wolf-blooded and human, having a human identity can give you reason to keep in touch with them. Packs can be businesses, with a few werewolves in charge and a couple wolf-blooded in the know and many human employees with no idea there's a supernatural aspect to their workplace. They can be criminal organizations where only the 'made men,' mostly wolf-blooded, know the truth about the bosses. Taking the human side of that out doesn't give you much of an anchor in the fleshly side of the game. It's still something you can do, though, you'll just likely hover around lower Harmony.</div> </div> </div> </div>Would it be the same if i either make more common wolf blooded? Or just not being so strict with lunacy? I mean i dont dislike human characters or storylines but i do thinks is more fun to see the variety of reactions that normal people have to the supernatural than play secret identity card.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/847509-looking-to-get-back-into-the-game?p=847538#post847538\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd really suggest against starting a game before a first change if you want to toss out the identity of the player character, but that's up to you. The fiction in the second edition book basically talks about a pack of werewolves who have to give up their identities for the hunt, and it's not an easy thing to do.<br /> <br /> Hope we can help with your game.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thing is while the werewolf game was awesome.....then we tried changeling the lost, players were all up for it leaving their somewhat hate of NWoD for giving things a try, and the game was awful. Plain simply awful. The players and DM had to many preconceptions about Dreaming that they push in lost and the game was weird inconsistent hybrid of a mess. Ex: ogres couldnt have entitlements because ogres CANNOT be noble, and most ogres had to be cannibals too (i dont mean the flaw, i mean biologically only able to eat human flesh).<br /> <br /> So i wasnt thinking a game when the player just went through their first change as to present them with the setting from the get go. So they cant have any misconpceptions about how the game works from Apocalypse.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bunyip</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/847509-looking-to-get-back-into-the-game?p=847570#post847570\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It sounds like most (all) of your perceptions are from 1st edition. Have a read through 2nd edition, it fixes the problems people had with aimlessness and 'what do werewolves do'. It should explain most of what you're asking here.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, i started with forsaken but never got around to finish the line. And yeah i have being reading the new Core but it wasnt a question about what do forsaken do but more how do they do what they do?<br /> <br /> For example another question is from 2ed, the hunt. I get, and i love symbolism, the synergy to it but, DM wise, what should i do if there is not one of each tribe on the game? Does the hunt function the same? Should they ask the a member from the tribes`s choosen prey to do the ritual for them? Should i avoid using those kind of enemy so the players arent in an unfair encounter?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-15T14:13:49-07:00","id":847855,"title":"","content":"Yeah there were a lot of changes and things cleared up as the line went on and especially in the shift to second edition.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/847509-looking-to-get-back-into-the-game?p=847822#post847822\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ahh so its a tribute based thing. When Spirits give essense to their superiors for protection? What about hybrid spirits, i remember reading they were abomination in 1st edition but it seems that now they are pretty common in 2nd?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah it's a tribute thing, basically. The magath are mixes of two or more concepts that don't fit together. As spirits evolve they can get more Resonances, so a night spirit might get a resonance of owl, and predators. But if the spirit was starved it might have eaten a bunch of computer spirits and become a magath of Night and Computers, and even more to add Trees to that. They're outcasts in the spirit courts and don't have the safety those courts can offer.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So how does a new pack gets a territory? I mean, it is in setting for a more experience pack to say &quot;yeah....take that part of our territory, we are overworked as it is&quot; </div> </div> </div>Yes, it can definitely happen. Especially if a territory is especially large or it's part of a protectorate, or there are a lot of loci that need watching and it's impossible for a single pack to do it all. Otherwise you can just find territories on your own. There might even be some challenging over them, which can be good fodder for plots, but it's not as if every square mile of the world has been claimed, there's plenty of space out there that is unclaimed and probably some places that have never been touched by werewolves.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I meant rank as in renown. For example i remember reading a tale in Signs of the moon, the elodoth chapter, in which 2 werewolves went to a higher ranked Elodoth for a dispute between a wolf blooded. Which always seem contrived to me, wouldnt challenge be more straight forward? </div> </div> </div>Werewolf isn't all about physical ability. That's certainly a part of it but wisdom is also appreciated, being one of the five renown and all. Beating up others doesn't solve problems or make decisions. The specific story you were referring to was because that elodoth was, basically, the ruler of all that land and likely the most powerful werewolf around, as well as being an elodoth, known to be the judges of the werewolves. His renown wasn't the only factor, but their situation was clearly a unique and odd one. All decisions don't have to be taken up to them, but the situation was about two packs claiming 'possession' of a wolf-blooded. If they decided to go the violent route, it's possible someone would have died or both packs would have gotten involved and their ally packs and spirits and so forth.<br /> <br /> Basically it's like human justice. You can go over to your neighbor and beat the shit out of him but that's not guaranteed to solve your problem, and might even make things worse. While going to an external source of judgment might not give you the best result, it is meant to at least be unbiased and, providing everyone agrees, a final solution.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Would it be the same if i either make more common wolf blooded? Or just not being so strict with lunacy? I mean i dont dislike human characters or storylines but i do thinks is more fun to see the variety of reactions that normal people have to the supernatural than play secret identity card. </div> </div> </div>You can but it's still eschewing the humanity side of things. It's a viable choice, low Harmony doesn't mean your character is unplayable anymore, but having a family or a group of friends doesn't have to be a Peter Parker-esque juggling sequence where you're constantly lying to people.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Thing is while the werewolf game was awesome.....then we tried changeling the lost, players were all up for it leaving their somewhat hate of NWoD for giving things a try, and the game was awful. Plain simply awful. The players and DM had to many preconceptions about Dreaming that they push in lost and the game was weird inconsistent hybrid of a mess. Ex: ogres couldnt have entitlements because ogres CANNOT be noble, and most ogres had to be cannibals too (i dont mean the flaw, i mean biologically only able to eat human flesh).<br /> <br /> So i wasnt thinking a game when the player just went through their first change as to present them with the setting from the get go. So they cant have any misconpceptions about how the game works from Apocalypse. </div> </div> </div>I understand, if you're running with the same people you might want to do that. Apocalypse and Forsaken are very, very different games in setting, play-style and mechanics. Some people new to the games tend to downplay certain things, so getting rid of human packmates or not having them be a part of things is, again, entirely viable.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yes, i started with forsaken but never got around to finish the line. And yeah i have being reading the new Core but it wasnt a question about what do forsaken do but more how do they do what they do?<br /> <br /> For example another question is from 2ed, the hunt. I get, and i love symbolism, the synergy to it but, DM wise, what should i do if there is not one of each tribe on the game? Does the hunt function the same? Should they ask the a member from the tribes`s choosen prey to do the ritual for them? Should i avoid using those kind of enemy so the players arent in an unfair encounter? </div> </div> </div>Werewolves hunt what needs to be hunted, or what they want to hunt. The sacred prey is just the favorite prey of a tribe, it doesn't mean other tribes can't hunt it or are going to fail at it. Some of the hunts might be harder, like a Storm Lord leading the hunt gives a really nice benefit to finding Claimed. But it's not something that's going to make or break the hunt. Gifts and other supernatural abilities can compensate, and even mundane skills can help.<br /> <br /> As some advice, when it comes to the first hunt I wouldn't go easy on them, either. Don't make it a completely weak being that's going to get torn to shreds in one round. Werewolves are tough. Don't have it focus fire on one person or do a lot of aggravated damage and everyone should be okay.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-16T04:52:05-07:00","id":848081,"title":"","content":"The bonuses a werewolf of a certain tribe gets when performing siskur-dah for their favoured prey aren't all that massive. They help, sure, but you're not at a huge disadvantage if you have to make do without them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}